From 613196a267fb68552b8d93f852e8c59053ade67e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josep Torra <n770galaxy@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 09:57:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 11/15] ##arch: Emulate the USSE forms pinned by hardware
 probes

Client fills on an SGX545 ran small programs of our own to settle what the
instruction text left open, and the ESIL now follows the answers:

- test group: the sign test p is >= 0 and t is true in either join;
  iadd32/iaddu32 add the low half of src1, sign- or zero-extended, to the
  32-bit src2 (the plain 32-bit sum was wrong), and the unsigned add's n is
  never true; isub16/isubu16 subtract both 16-bit lanes and test the low
  one (chan0)
- shl/shr/asr/rol by a register amount: 32 and over give 0 or the sign,
  rol takes the amount mod 32
- ima16 ..., u16, u16: a * b +/- c in each 16-bit lane
- the index register: "mov i.l, src" and moves through r[il + #n] and
  sa[il + #n] (i.l bits 10 and up ignored), as chains of conditionals
  over the profile's registers; il/ih join the register profile
- loads and stores with a register offset: the product of its 16-bit
  halves, in bytes; a store to an unaligned address does not land
- ldr masks a register index to 14 bits
- mov repeats run in ascending order, so overlapping ranges propagate,
  and take one predicate for the whole instruction
- fmad rounds the product, then the sum (not fused), and flushes denormal
  inputs, product and result to a zero of their sign; the float sources of
  frcp/frsq/fsqrt are flushed too

8 new test/db/esil/usse tests; 84.2% of the microkernel's words have ESIL
(was 81.0%).

